PROJECT TextEmbedder

# Vocabulary: 30 domain words (billing / technical / sales)
# Input: bag-of-words binary presence vector derived from raw ticket text.
# A 1 in column bow_X means the word X appears in the ticket.
VECTOR TicketBOW[30]
  bow_account: Score
  bow_amount: Score
  bow_api: Score
  bow_cancel: Score
  bow_charge: Score
  bow_connection: Score
  bow_contract: Score
  bow_crash: Score
  bow_demo: Score
  bow_discount: Score
  bow_enterprise: Score
  bow_error: Score
  bow_features: Score
  bow_fix: Score
  bow_help: Score
  bow_invoice: Score
  bow_issue: Score
  bow_login: Score
  bow_need: Score
  bow_network: Score
  bow_payment: Score
  bow_plan: Score
  bow_pricing: Score
  bow_problem: Score
  bow_refund: Score
  bow_reset: Score
  bow_server: Score
  bow_slow: Score
  bow_timeout: Score
  bow_upgrade: Score
END

# Two-layer dense network: BoW[30] -> Dense(8,relu) -> Dense(1,sigmoid)
# The 8-unit hidden layer is the learned text projection (text embedding).
# Output routing_signal in [0,1]: billing ~ 0.1, technical ~ 0.5, sales ~ 0.9.
#
# The output is declared Vector[1] and not Score, and that is the whole
# point of this example being a *composite*: the next model in the
# pipeline (route_classifier) takes `VECTOR Signal[1]`, and a Dense(1)
# layer emits exactly that — one element. Declared as `Score`, the
# published interface said `kind: Score` where the consumer said
# `kind: VECTOR`, and `matrixai typecheck` rejected a pipeline that
# routes 9 out of 9 tickets correctly. The fix is to say what it emits,
# not to teach the checker that a Score and a VECTOR[1] are the same
# thing: that would weaken the check for every other composite.
# The [0,1] meaning is not lost — the consumer declares the field as
# `routing_signal: Score` inside its own vector.
NETWORK TextEncoder
  INPUT TicketBOW
  LAYER Dense units=8 activation=relu
  LAYER Dense units=1 activation=sigmoid
  OUTPUT routing_signal: Vector[1]
END

GRAPH
  TicketBOW -> TextEncoder
END
